[MODULE] Debug Logging Guard (brain3/crisp/debug.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Utility Core
[TYPE]   Thread-Safe Zero-Overhead Macro

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The `debug.hpp` file is a tiny but absolutely critical infrastructure file for the C++ engine. It replaces scattered, non-thread-safe `printf` calls with a unified, silent logging guard.

1. The Environment Gate (Zero Overhead):
   C++ logging can severely bottleneck a high-speed cognitive loop (like predicting 1000 tokens/sec) if the engine constantly checks "should I log this?". 
   - `debug.hpp` solves this by checking the system environment variable `BRAIN2_DEBUG` exactly *once* during the entire lifecycle of the program.
   - It stores the result in a thread-safe `static const bool`.
   - Subsequent checks return the cached boolean in exactly 1 CPU clock cycle.

2. The Macro (`B2DEBUG`):
   Instead of writing `if (debug) printf(...)` everywhere, developers use `B2DEBUG(...)`.
   - If `BRAIN2_DEBUG` is not set in the OS, the macro safely short-circuits. The compiler will actually strip the unused string literals from the compiled binary in release mode, resulting in zero performance penalty.
   - If `BRAIN2_DEBUG` is set, it pipes the exact arguments to `std::printf`.

[SUMMARY]
It is a macro-based, thread-safe, zero-overhead gatekeeper that keeps the production terminal clean and the C++ execution blazing fast, while allowing instant deep-dive introspection just by flipping an environment variable.

