[MODULE] Logic Engine (brain3/crisp/logic_engine.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   Symbolic Execution Engine / Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The `logic_engine.hpp` module is the "hands" of the Basal Ganglia. When the Basal Ganglia selects a discrete operation (e.g., `Op::MATH_ADD` or `Op::BIND_QUERY`), it delegates the physical execution of that operation to the Logic Engine, which manipulates the `Scratchpad`.

1. Grounded Arithmetic:
   The Brain does not inherently know how to multiply. The Logic Engine implements math using "Grounded Procedures" discovered by the synthesis engine:
   - Addition is implemented via the successor atom (`x + 1` repeated `y` times).
   - Multiplication is repeated addition.
   - Exponentiation is repeated multiplication.
   This means the Brain physically performs the mathematical steps rather than blindly relying on the CPUs ALU. (It gracefully falls back to native CPU hardware acceleration only when values exceed `4096` to prevent hanging).

2. Memory Interfaces:
   The Logic Engine translates high-level intents into precise vector queries. 
   - `BIND_QUERY`: Asks the Binding Memory database a question and writes the `confidence` score back to the scratchpad.
   - `RETRIEVE`: Executes a full continuous-discrete dot-product search against the Hippocampus (Episodic Memory).
   - `CHAIN_FOLLOW`: An iterative Breadth-First Search (BFS) bounded to 10 hops. If the Brain wants to know the long-term consequence of an action, it uses this to hop across the "causes" relation in Binding Memory without triggering an O(N^depth) recursive explosion.

3. Holographic Context Calculation:
   After every single operation, the Logic Engine recalculates the `context` of the Scratchpad. It binds the value inside a slot with a vector representing the slot itself (`val * SLOT_SUBJECT`), sums all slots together, and L2 normalizes them. This guarantees the Scratchpad always collapses into a single 64D vector that the Brain can "see."

[SUMMARY]
The Logic Engine is a heavily optimized Symbolic ALU. It executes exact math, queries complex vector databases, and collapses the discrete results back into continuous geometry for the neural layer to process.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Locality-Sensitive Hashing / Cognitive TLB (brain3/fuzzy/lsh.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Neural Core
[TYPE]   O(1) Vector Address Cache

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The `lsh.hpp` module implements a "Cognitive Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB)". Just like an Operating System uses a Page Table to map Virtual Memory to Physical Memory instantly, the Brain uses LSH to map a continuous "logical thought" vector to a discrete physical BMU address in O(1) time.
It generates a 64-bit binary hash using random hyperplanes. If two thought vectors are geometrically similar, they will likely generate the exact same 64-bit integer hash, allowing instant cache hits without needing an expensive O(N) distance search over the entire SOM grid.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Global Memoization (brain3/crisp/memoization.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   Compute Cache

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
A simple but highly effective global cache that stores string-to-string (math/logic) and string-to-vector (database query) results. Tracks cache hit/miss rates. Prevents the brain from re-calculating or re-querying known facts during complex reasoning chains.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Policy Engine (brain3/crisp/policy_engine.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   Neurosymbolic Executive / Means-Ends Solver

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
This is the absolute core of the Crisp logical layer. It executes Means-Ends Analysis to solve symbolic goals.

1. Tabling and Cycle Guards:
   When searching for a proof, it maintains a `memo` (tabling) to avoid re-proving subgoals and a `seen` set to instantly break infinite logical cycles.

2. Groundability Proposer:
   Instead of trying random logical rules, it sorts candidate rules by their "groundability" (how likely their inputs are to be known facts).

3. The "Conjecture -> Verify -> Admit" Loop:
   When learning a new policy (rule), it first mathematically composes a Conjecture. It then runs the new rule against the exact step-by-step derivation. It only Admits the rule into `PolicyMemory` if the numeric results are identical down to a `1e-6` tolerance. This completely prevents the fuzzy neural layer from poisoning the Brains strict logic.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Predictive Coding Layer (brain3/fuzzy/predictive_coding.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Neural/Fuzzy Core
[TYPE]   Prediction Error Wrapper

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
A simple wrapper that forces the Brain to operate purely on "Surprise." It computes `error = actual - prediction`. The downstream modules only learn from or react to the `error` signal, saving massive amounts of compute by completely ignoring perfectly predictable sensory input.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Predictor Engine (brain3/fuzzy/predictor.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Neural Core
[TYPE]   AWD-LSTM with LM Head

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The core temporal sequence learner. It predicts the *next* state of the Brain.

1. AWD-LSTM Regularization:
   Uses inverted input dropout to prevent the recurrent network from overfitting to exact sensory noise.
   
2. Adam/SGD Split:
   The core LSTM uses standard SGD, but the Language Modeling (LM) projection head uses Adam with Weight Tying. This split learning rate architecture ensures the projection head can rapidly learn word unigram distributions (the bias) while the LSTM slowly learns deep contextual grammar.

3. Within-Chunk Exact Attention:
   Implements a mathematically exact self-attention mechanism over the current temporal chunk (h2[0..t]), allowing gradients to flow back through both the residual connection AND the attention weights `alpha`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Procedural Memory (brain3/crisp/procedural_memory.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   Habit / Action Sequence Store

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
Stores sequences of operations (`std::vector<Op>`) that successfully solved a problem in the past. 
When the Brain encounters a similar context vector (`cos_sim > 0.9f`), it instantly retrieves the entire sequence of actions instead of spending compute re-deriving the solution via Means-Ends analysis. Over time, slow deliberate reasoning physically hardens into fast, unconscious habit.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Synthesis Proposer (brain3/crisp/proposer.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   Discriminative Feature Learner

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
A native C++ port of the synthesis feature learner. When synthesizing new algorithms, it calculates the variance of features across winning spaces. Features with high variance (they successfully separate good spaces from bad spaces) are assigned high discriminative weight, allowing the Brain to rapidly zero-in on correct algorithmic structures.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Reasoning Ops (brain3/crisp/reasoning_ops.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   Structure Mapping Primitives

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
Implements `analogy_score`, the core algorithm for Structure Mapping. It takes two sets of relationships and an entity mapping, and returns a score based on how many relations perfectly align. If the mapping forces an inconsistent relation (e.g., mapping "causes" to both "prevents" and "creates" simultaneously), it instantly rejects it (returns -1).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Reasoning Engine (brain3/crisp/reasoning.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   System 2 (Slow) Thinking

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The `reasoning.hpp` module implements deliberate, step-by-step System 2 thinking. While the Neural layers instantly pattern-match (System 1), this engine chains logical operations together using the `Scratchpad` and `Symbolic` engines.
It supports conditional execution (only execute a step if a vector similarity threshold is met) and convergence loops (repeating a chain of reasoning until the output vector stops changing).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Refuter (brain3/crisp/refuter.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   Aggressive Oracle Verifier

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
This module actively tries to break hypothesized rules. Given a candidate rule and a ground-truth oracle over a range of integers, it hunts for the exact point where they disagree.
It characterizes the exact "Valid Scope" of a rule (e.g., "holds for ALL n in [0,10] U [15,20]"). It acts as the Brains harsh scientific method, completely rejecting "almost right" neural hallucinations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Regularity Detector (brain3/crisp/regularity.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   Domain Boundary Mapper

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
A pure mathematical layer that determines if a domain *can* be verified. It attempts to fit linear and power laws to training points via least squares. If the relative error on held-out data is low, it tells the Brain that this domain has a "Checkable Regularity." 
This prevents the Brain from wasting massive compute trying to find exact logical rules in purely chaotic or random domains.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Scratchpad Memory (brain3/crisp/scratchpad.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   Unbounded External Tape

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The `scratchpad.hpp` module allows the Brain to bypass the strict 7-item limit of Working Memory. It acts like a piece of paper for a human doing math.
1. Named Slots & History: It can write vectors to named slots and maintains an 8-step history for every slot, allowing instant backtracking.
2. Stack: Implements a push/pop stack for recursive reasoning algorithms.
3. Possibility Trees: Implements a heuristic search tree. The Brain can branch off into hypothetical states, calculate a heuristic cost (distance to goal), and jump to the "best child" branch, allowing physical implementation of A* / Means-Ends Analysis within the vector space.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Self-Model (brain3/fuzzy/self_model.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Neural/Fuzzy Core
[TYPE]   Internal State Observer / Introspection

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The `self_model.hpp` module allows the Brain to look at itself. Every tick, it captures a 10D vector of its internal state (valence, arousal, salience, prediction error, working memory load, etc.).
1. Self-SOM: It maps these 10D internal states onto a mini-SOM. Over time, physical clusters form representing discrete moods (e.g., a cluster for "I am calm and focused", a cluster for "I am panicked and surprised").
2. Identity Drift: It maintains a running mean of every state it has ever experienced (its "Identity"). It compares its current state to its Identity to calculate a "Drift" score. If Drift is high, the Brain physically recognizes that it is acting anomalously compared to its "typical self."

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Graph SOM (brain3/fuzzy/som.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Neural/Fuzzy Core
[TYPE]   Dynamic Navigable Small World Graph

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The `som.hpp` module is the Brains primary geometric clustering engine. Unlike a traditional rigid 2D grid SOM, this is a Navigable Small World (NSW) Graph. 
1. Graded Activation: Instead of returning a 1-hot "Winning Neuron," it returns a graded activation map where the BMU (Best Matching Unit) is 1.0, and nearby neighbors decay softly. This allows the downstream LSTM to learn complex interpolations ("sort of like X and Y").
2. Dynamic Rewiring: Every 100 hits, a neuron tries to forge a new graph edge to another random neuron that is semantically close in vector space. The physical layout of the brain rewires itself based on experience.
3. Hebbian Nudging: When the Crisp layer discovers a strict logical truth (e.g. "Dog ISA Animal"), it calls `hebbian_nudge()`, which physically pulls the two neurons closer together in the geometric space.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Sparse LSTM Layer (brain3/fuzzy/sparse_lstm.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Neural Core
[TYPE]   LSH-Routed Recurrent Neural Network

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The `sparse_lstm.hpp` module solves the O(N^2) scaling problem of massive LSTMs.
1. LSH Routing: Before a forward pass, the input vector is hashed by the Cognitive TLB. This hash maps to a specific "bucket" of neurons. Only those specific neurons wake up and perform matrix math. The rest of the network stays dormant, reducing compute by orders of magnitude.
2. Sparse Backprop: The backward pass only calculates gradients and updates weights for the exact rows that were active during the forward pass.
3. Decoupled Weight Decay: It implements AdamW-style decoupled weight decay, but strictly bounds it to the active rows to prevent catastrophic forgetting of dormant concepts.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Symbolic Binding (brain3/crisp/symbolic.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Crisp Core
[TYPE]   Stable Concept Registry

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The `symbolic.hpp` module maps rigid strings (like "+", "-", "=", "0", "1") to stable concept vectors in the same dimensional space as the SOM.
Unlike natural language words (which drift constantly as their meaning changes), mathematical symbols must remain stable. 
However, it implements a `ground()` method. If the Brain is told "2 + 2 = 4" while looking at 4 apples, the `ground` method very slightly pulls the vector for "4" towards the visual vector for "apples". This is how the Brain learns what abstract numbers actually mean in the physical world.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[MODULE] Hierarchical Working Memory (brain3/fuzzy/working_mem.hpp)
[LAYER]  C++ Neural/Fuzzy Core
[TYPE]   Spiking Prefrontal Cortex

[DEEP ARCHITECTURE & MECHANICS]
The `working_mem.hpp` module simulates the Prefrontal Cortex using a rostro-caudal hierarchy of Leaky Integrate-and-Fire (LIF) buffers.
1. Tiered Promotion:
   - Tier 0 (Sensory): 7 slots, fast decay.
   - Tier 1 (Semantic): 3 slots, medium decay.
   - Tier 2 (Goal): 1 slot, very slow decay.
   When Tier 0 fills up, the most active concept isn`t deleted; it is "promoted" to Tier 1. This naturally extracts long-term abstract context from high-speed sensory streams.
2. Causal Disruption: If the Brain experiences massive Prediction Error (Surprise), this module instantly looks at the working memory slots. Any slot that was highly correlated with the error is violently suppressed (anti-Hebbian un-learning). The Brain physically deletes the thoughts that led to the wrong prediction.

