[ARCHITECTURE ENHANCEMENT] Brain-Mouth Neurosymbolic Disambiguation & JSON Contract
[DATE] 2026-08-12
[LAYER] C++ JNI Adapter (`BrainJNI.cpp`) <-> Java Adapter (`BrainInterface.java`, `LLMAdapter.java`)
[PROBLEM] 
  1. The neural predictor (`think()`) was previously in the direct factual response path, leading to small-model memorization/hallucination instead of crisp database lookup.
  2. The Brain-Mouth JSON schema had ambiguous token counts and no machine-readable way to differentiate crisp factual logic from stochastic neural daydreams.
  3. Java side was using fragile regex parsing for JSON payloads.
[CHANGES & MECHANICS]
  1. Routed factual `LOOKUP` directly to `BrainQLExecutor` (Crisp Symbolic Store) with `think()` completely removed from the factual path.
  2. Established rigid JSON schema with explicit machine-readable fields:
     `{"op": "...", "subj": "...", "rel": "...", "obj": "...", "objType": "atomic"|"freetext", "result": "...", "verified": bool, "known": bool, "source": "..."}`
  3. Added `json_escape` helper in `BrainJNI.cpp` to prevent malformed text serialization crashes.
  4. Upgraded Java parsing layer from regex to `org.json.JSONObject`.
  5. Implemented tone-branching in `MOUTH_SYSTEM_BQL` prompt: plain assertions when `known: true`, mandatory hedging ("I was just thinking about...") when `known: false`.

